index 6341bc5..32332f6 100644
--- a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
@@ -91,6 +91,6 @@ Delete is a commit-point operation too, and its cost is paid lazily, off the cri
 
    A multipart upload that has ended — aborted, or completed — **leaves the store at a terminal delete**, in the same repair-loop pass, before the obligations are walked. The custodian walks the upload's own staging entries — the residue of a part whose write stopped before it committed — writing the grace record of every position an entry names, under the upload's own identity, and deleting the entry in a later commit; nothing refills that range once the upload has ended, so the entries left are the progress. An entry can name more positions than one batch reads (a wide erasure scheme, or a damaged placement), so the walk keeps how far it has got inside one entry in one small record of the custodian's own per upload, written in the batch whose grace records it counts under a condition on its exact value — and put only while the upload's record is unchanged, so a batch that lands after another drainer has ended the upload leaves none behind — and removed with that entry or, at the latest, with the upload. It names the first entry the last batch left that the walk can still work on — whatever it covered of it — and the next batch walks that entry first, so an entry held for the operator ahead of it never stalls it. A batch that writes no grace record and deletes no entry can only move that record: it is outside the pass's batch budget and ends the upload's turn for the pass, so uploads whose held entries keep moving it cannot starve the rest. Entries held for the operator stay in the range, so a second small record per upload says where the walk's next read starts: a batch that marks and deletes nothing and holds something moves it past the held entries at the head of what it read — leaving the first record on a held entry rather than moving it to the entry it has only read again, which the next batch reads first — and once nothing is left after it the walk is back at the start of the range and the record is deleted. Every entry is therefore read once a lap however many are held ahead of it, and each held entry is named to the operator again every lap. That record is a position, never evidence — what an entry's deletion needs does not change with it, and one that names nothing of the upload's range is named to the operator and read as the start. It is written in a commit of its own, outside the pass's batch budget so that uploads with held entries cannot starve the rest, at most twice per upload a pass, put only while the upload's record is unchanged, and removed at the latest with the upload. Once a pass finds the range empty and no obligation of the upload left, one batch, conditioned on the upload's record exactly as read, deletes the record and the slots its parts reserved, deletes the reservation of its segment group unless a published object adopted the group, and lowers the admission count by one: of two drainers that race to it, one commits and the other's batch is refused, so the count goes down exactly once and an admission slot is released once. A **completed** upload's record is the tombstone that answers a retried completion with the published result, so it is kept, and its admission slot with it, until its tombstone window has passed; nothing measures that window yet, so the deployed custodian keeps every completed upload's record for now. For an **aborted** upload only, a record whose placement cannot be trusted — a part or a staging entry whose placement is not one server per fragment, or a staging entry that will not decode under a key that still names its chunk — is retired **by chunk id**: every fragment of that chunk that the D servers' own listings show gets a grace record, and for a staging entry that decodes so does every position it names, before the record goes. This runs only on a pass that sees every server the operator configured, the condition GC already runs under, since a listing that misses a server would leave that server's fragments with neither record nor grace record; on any other pass the record is held and named to the operator, and nothing is lost while it waits. The positions a record names itself are a fixed order, so the drain resumes inside them from its cursor across as many batches as they need. A listing is no such order — a fragment that drops out of it moves every later one — so a batch that reaches a chunk's listed fragments reads all of them, and progress is counted a whole chunk at a time; a chunk listed with more fragments than one batch reads is held and named to the operator as needing a human. A batch lists the fleet at most once, so a pass lists it at most once per batch it commits, plus once per aborted upload whose damaged record is held after its listing. One gap is stated rather than hidden: a write the aborted upload had authorized that lands after the listing, at a position the damaged record does not name, has neither record nor grace record, and GC keeps it — kept for good, never lost, never reclaimed without an operator. A part record that will not decode names no chunk in its key, so it is held for a human, and the upload stays until one acts. A grace record the walk wrote over a position whose write never landed stays as well, since that write may still land there.
 
-   A multipart upload whose client went silent — never aborted, never completed — is ended by the **multipart reaper** (proposal 0016 decision 6), which runs in the same repair-loop pass, after reconstruction and before the terminal deletes and the obligation walk, so an upload it ends is drained in the same pass. A reconstruction failure does not skip them: the pass still reports it, but only after the reaper and the drain have had their turn, so a fault reading the repair queue cannot keep abandoned uploads holding their admission slots. It first compares the budget profile the admission record stores with its own and, if they differ or the record will not decode, stops before reading any upload and names the mismatch for an operator — and the terminal deletes and the obligation walk do not run in that pass either, since they build the staged references that profile sizes, so no upload's records change in that pass. It reads the admission record once more as the last thing it does, just before those legs run, so a record installed or replaced under another profile while it worked — a first upload created by a differently sized gateway — stops them the same way. With no admission record at all nothing was ever admitted, so it judges no upload. If the record is still missing at its last read while an upload is listed — or the listing cannot be read — the record is lost and the profile those uploads were admitted under is unknown, so the pass is refused as for a mismatch, and the terminal deletes and the obligation walk do not run either. A record that was there at the first read and is gone at the last is lost in the same way — nothing deletes it — and refuses the pass the same way, whatever the pass did before that read. A refusal at the first read says that the reaper refused before acting; one at the last read of a pass that had judged says how many uploads it had already ended — they stay ended, under the profile the first read matched — and the pass's counts are reported either way, as they are when that last read fails. A first upload created under its own profile while the pass works is left for the next pass, and that pass reports that it did not finish rather than that nothing is left. It lists the uploads in key order, at most a fixed number per pass, and keeps where it stopped in a position of its own, `custodian:reaper-cursor`, so the next pass goes on from there and every upload is judged once a lap (a stored position that is not an upload key a backend accepts is named and replaced by the head, never handed to the store). When that number runs out it reads one entry further, judging none, so a listing that ends exactly there is known to have ended. A read of the listing the store cannot answer — a page of it, or that one entry — holds the rest of the listing for that pass, named for an operator, and sends the next pass back to the head rather than to the read that failed, so a fault that lasts never keeps the uploads listed before it from being judged again. The position also records whether the lap so far is clean — nothing ended, contended or held since its head — so the pass that finishes a clean lap reports that nothing is left, and one that finishes any other lap reports that it did not finish, and a longer listing still reaches the first report once a whole lap finds nothing to do; the admission count does not bound the listing, since a lowered limit or uploads created and deleted between two pages can list more uploads than the count, and neither is damage. It judges only an **open** one stamped under the clock it reads. An upload stamped under another clock is named for an operator and never judged; while it is open only an operator can end it, so it is held, and once it has ended its teardown runs as any other's, since none of it reads a timestamp. An upload whose creation or newest progress is stamped later than the reaper's own clock by more than the clock-skew budget is held too, rather than kept for as long as the stamp stays ahead. An open upload is ended when it is **idle** — its newest progress (its creation, its newest committed part, its newest in-flight slot reservation) is older than the idle window and no slot or staging lease of it is still live — or when it is **over age**, older than the residency ceiling however live it is. It reads the slot range before the part summaries, so a part committing between the two reads is seen in one or both, never neither. An upload whose records it cannot read or decode — a part summary under a key that names no part included — is held and named for an operator, and the pass goes on to the next and to the terminal deletes and the obligation walk. Ending an upload is the one commit a client abort also makes — the upload moved to aborting under a condition on its exact record, with the obligation owing everything it staged — and the idle commit also pins every slot of the upload as read, present or absent, so a renewal or release of a slot it read, or a reservation still standing when the commit lands, makes the commit fail and the upload is judged again next pass; the over-age commit pins nothing. A commit that fails because the upload's teardown obligation already exists beside its unchanged record is damage rather than contention, since no writer leaves that state: the upload is held and named. A commit whose outcome the store could not report is neither a success nor a failure: the upload may already have been ended, and the drain of that same pass then tears it down, so it is never reported as left in place and never counted as ended. It is named for an operator and counted on its own, the pass reports that it could not account for everything, the commit is not sent again, and the next pass reads which way it went. One gap is stated rather than hidden: a request that reserves a slot the reaper read free and commits its part, both between the reads and the idle commit, leaves every slot as the reaper read it, so the commit lands and that upload is ended together with the part it has just committed. Closing that needs a change to the upload protocol (proposal 0016) that this release does not make; it is a known gap, and the follow-up that closes it must land before uploads are created in production. Each commit is measured against the batch budgets before it is sent and never split. An operator can run exactly one such pass and exit with `wyrd custodian --reap-abandoned-uploads`: it needs no `--endpoints`, reads and writes metadata only, and drains what it ended record by record, holding for a whole-fleet pass anything that could only be retired by listing the fleet; it exits non-zero when anything was held, a commit's outcome is unknown or the profile check refused, so an open upload under another clock keeps it failing until an operator ends that upload. It is refused before it opens the store when given a flag that selects work it does not run — `--endpoints`, `--ids`, `--failure-domains` or `--gc-expired-pending` — rather than ignoring the flag, and when its `--data-dir` holds no local metadata store — no file there, or an empty one — which it never creates: a store made on the spot would be reported as holding nothing to reap. Its last line names the store it ran on: the local file, or the TiKV PD endpoints or the FoundationDB cluster file it connected with.
